Body:
Correction after checking SolidJS implementation: the original root-cause wording was too strong.

`mapArray` itself runs the mapper inside `untrack`, so simply adding `title: session.title` inside that mapper would **not** create the subscription I described.

A more accurate diagnosis is: the v2 tab bar resolves session info in a `mapArray` mapper that is only driven by the local tab list, while the title change comes from the external session store. Because the mapper is untracked, any session-derived snapshot created there can become stale. The robust fix is to keep only a session accessor in the mapper, then resolve `info` and read `info.title` inside the reactive `tabsEnriched()`/`<For each>` path:

```ts
const base = mapArray(
  () => tabsStore,
  (tab) => {
    const sync = serverSync.createDirSyncContext(tab.dir)
    return { ...tab, info: () => sync.session.get(tab.sessionId) }
  },
)

return () =>
  base().flatMap((tab) => {
    const info = tab.info()
    return info ? [{ ...tab, info, title: info.title }] : []
  })
```

This makes the `each` expression track `info.title` directly, so the tab bar reacts to `session.updated` events that change the generated title.

@rekram1-node Thanks for the response. Just to clarify — are you saying that `@ai-sdk/azure` with the Responses API isn't a fully supported configuration yet, and the encrypted content issue is a known limitation? Or is there something else I should look at on my side?

For context: the non-native AI SDK fallback path is what's being used here (since the native runtime rejects `@ai-sdk/azure`), and it breaks consistently after 3-4 tool-calling turns with `store: false`.

Is there a recommended workaround or configuration to make this work reliably? We're using this in an enterprise context where `@ai-sdk/azure` is the only option (all traffic routes through an Azure OpenAI-compatible gateway that enforces `store: false`). Without a fix, GPT-5.x models are effectively unusable from within OpenCode for anything beyond trivial single-turn requests.
